1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,640
Hi, in this video we'll use Burp Suite to test for reflected XSS manually.

2
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:17,520
Reflected cross-site scripting, or XSS, occurs when an application receives data in an HTTP

3
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:22,320
request and includes that data within the immediate response in an unsafe way.

4
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:28,720
While Burp Scanner can detect reflected XSS, you can also manually test applications for

5
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:35,920
reflected XSS using Burp Repeater. Burp Repeater enables you to manipulate HTTP requests directly,

6
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:41,680
making it easier to test whether reflected input is adequately sanitized or filtered server-side.

7
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:48,400
If you'd like to follow along, you can use the lab Reflected XSS into HTML context with nothing

8
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:55,680
encoded from our Web Security Academy. Before we start, we'll identify a request that reflects input.

9
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:01,360
If you'd like to know more about this, we have a separate topic on identifying reflected input.

10
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:06,320
Once you've identified a request, send it to Burp Repeater for further testing.

11
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,840
In this case, we have a request that uses the search.

12
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,080
Send the request and note the location of the reflected input and the context in which the

13
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:21,680
input is reflected, as this affects the potential XSS vectors you can use to construct an attack.

14
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:28,240
In this example, the input is reflected inside an HTML element.

15
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:32,640
In the Response panel, set the search term to be H1.

16
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,920
Click on the small cog and select Auto Scroll when text changes.

17
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:42,160
Change the canary to an XSS proof of concept attack.

18
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:47,920
For example, we can use the alert function by replacing the canary string with script alert1.

